artifact.self_contained_problem:
Call a function $f$ on a domain $\Omega \subset \mathbb{R}^2$ (or on a two-dimensional manifold) *partially analytic* if every level set $\{f = c\}$ is a real analytic curve, while $f$ itself is only required to have finite Sobolev regularity $H^s$ transversally to its level sets. The collection $\mathcal{P}(\Omega)$ of partially analytic functions is not closed under pointwise addition: the sum of two partially analytic functions generically fails to have real analytic level sets.

Identify a natural mathematical structure (for example, a groupoid, a category, a sheaf-theoretic or fibered structure, a non-linear topological space, or another suitable algebraic/geometric framework) that should replace the vector-space structure on $\mathcal{P}(\Omega)$, in such a way that:

(i) the structure restricts to the usual vector-space structure on subspaces of fully real analytic functions;
(ii) the structure is preserved by area-preserving diffeomorphisms acting by pullback (so that it is compatible with the symplectic / Hamiltonian setting of two-dimensional ideal hydrodynamics);
(iii) the structure interacts naturally with the Sobolev regularity profile across levels, allowing one to formulate well-posedness statements for two-dimensional incompressible Euler-type equations whose vorticity (or stream function) is partially analytic.

Equivalently: construct the correct ambient "space" of partially analytic functions and describe its operations.

